## The closest mathematical comparator

[Kuboyama, *How Many Labels Does Model Choice Need?*, arXiv
2609.18622v1, 16 September 2026](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.18622v1), is a
preprint. Sections 2–6 and 8–9, theorem proofs and the source archive were read.
Fixed predictions and confidence ranks define a finite-pool AUGRC decision.
Shared-label differences give exact, tie-aware stopping after adaptive queries.
A full-label covering LP bounds the minimum certificate within `K−1` labels.
That is hindsight sufficiency, not an acquisition strategy. Its near-all-label
acquisition result assumes independent random rankings and iid errors independent
of those rankings. It is not a general impossibility result for driving data.

Reported effort counts labels. Arbitrary detector matching, unreliable review
and complete analyst work are outside that demonstrated comparison. The paper
is CC BY 4.0. Its retained source archive contains manuscript and figure material;
the author-described executable companion was not located in the inspected
release. That is an access limitation, not proof that no companion exists.

**Our inference:** shared evidence, exact stopping and a small certificate are
insufficient differentiation by themselves. This reinforces Reiyah's retained
[conventional parity](../../research/value-disproof/0.1.0/README.md) and the
distinction between [204 simulated disclosures and a 148-disclosure hindsight
minimum](../../research/threshold-reference-screen/0.1.0/README.md).
It does not invalidate the existing conditional calculations.

